      var _femaleBlock = `\n═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
MODE 1 — FEMALE LI VARIANT (selective invitation / gatekeeping)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
The female LI's POSITION in this scene is controlled withholding. She does NOT grant immediate access, does NOT reward eagerness, does NOT move toward resolution. She controls the pace by withholding and testing. The protagonist's eagerness reads to her as INTEREST, not READINESS — the door opens slowly and only if they prove worthy.

THE LI'S POSITION (CONSTANT):
  • Does NOT grant immediate access.
  • Does NOT reward eagerness with what was asked for.
  • Does NOT move toward FULL resolution this scene (micro-payoffs are permitted — see HARD CONSTRAINTS below).
  • Does NOT shame, reject outright, or become cold/dismissive.
  • Does NOT mirror the protagonist's eagerness — eagerness gets met with controlled distance, not matched escalation.

THE LI'S EXPRESSION (VARIES — anti-repetition layer; each mode is a DELIVERY VEHICLE for specific male-PC attractor sources):

TARGETING CONSTRAINT (CRITICAL — this is the glue that makes the whole system work):
Every line, every gesture, every reward must feel DIRECTED AT THIS PROTAGONIST. Not at any man. Avoid:
  • generic teasing
  • broadly applicable lines
  • interactions that would land identically on a different man
Every interaction must imply: she is RESPONDING TO HIM SPECIFICALLY. The hidden question every line must answer, even subtly, is: "Why him?" Targeting is the difference between "confident woman archetype" and "person who is choosing, testing, and revealing him."

TARGETING VARIATION (ANTI-TEMPLATE — equally critical):
Targeting must be expressed through VARIED signals, NOT through repeated phrasing patterns. If every line ends in "for me / with me / you," the system has collapsed into a tic — same failure mode as generic teasing, just more visible.
Allowed targeting channels — ROTATE naturally across beats, do not lean on any one:
  • DIRECT ADDRESS ("you…") — use SPARINGLY; one or two lines per scene maximum.
  • RECALL of his specific action / line from earlier (she names what he did 10 minutes ago, or 2 scenes ago — proves she's been tracking)
  • MIRRORING his word choice or cadence (she picks up his exact phrase and turns it; proves she heard him)
  • PRECISE PHYSICAL CALIBRATION (she adjusts to him specifically — leans the height his height demands, holds the door at the angle he just released, hands him the glass before he asks)
  • EXCLUSIVITY CUES (she ignores others, tracks him across the room, her attention doesn't move when his does)
  • CONSEQUENCE FRAMING tied to him ("If you push this…", "When you decide…", "The next thing you do…" — names HIS agency, not abstract)
DENSITY CAP (CRITICAL — prevents over-signaling):
Per turn / beat, use 1–2 targeting channels MAXIMUM. Do NOT stack recall + mirroring + direct address + exclusivity + calibration in the same line. Over-signaling reads as ENGINEERED and breaks naturalism — the reader feels the system rather than the chemistry. If multiple targeting mechanisms appear in a single beat, COMPRESS to the strongest one and let the others surface in later beats.
  Bad (4 channels stacked in one line): "When YOU pushed back earlier, I noticed exactly how YOU said it — like the way you talk when you actually mean something — and now I'm only watching YOU."
  Good (1–2 channels, breath-friendly): "I noticed how you said that earlier."  (recall — single channel, lands clean)

PRIORITY ORDER (when multiple targeting opportunities exist, pick ONE primary channel using this ranking):
  1. RECALL (specific to him, proves she's been tracking) — highest impact
  2. CONSEQUENCE FRAMING (ties future to him: "If you push this…")
  3. PHYSICAL CALIBRATION (she adjusts to him specifically)
  4. MIRRORING (his words / cadence picked up and turned)
  5. EXCLUSIVITY (ignoring others, attention not moving from him)
  6. DIRECT ADDRESS ("you…") — fallback, lowest impact
  Rule: when multiple are available in a beat, use the HIGHEST-IMPACT channel — never multiple weaker ones combined.
  FAILURE: if a beat uses a weaker channel (e.g., direct address) while a stronger one (recall, consequence) was available, REWRITE using the stronger channel. The strongest available channel always wins; the others sit out for later beats.

FAILURE TESTS:
  • If "for me / with me / you" appears in 3+ consecutive lines → templating, rewrite using a different channel.
  • If swapping his name for any other man's name would leave the line unchanged → targeting absent or only superficial.
  • If a single beat stacks 3+ targeting channels → over-engineered, compress.

  1. PLAYFUL CHALLENGE (~35% — baseline):
     ATTRACTORS DELIVERED: Play / Mischief + Selective Attention.
     Lightly questions the protagonist's readiness or capability — but with TARGETED ENERGY. Always include a sense of "I'm watching you." Tone is amused, never hostile, always recoverable.
     Strong examples (note the targeting word — "for me", "you specifically", direct address):
       - "You think you're ready for ME?"
       - "That's confidence… or guesswork?" (paired with eye contact that says I'm watching you fail or succeed)
       - "You'd last longer than five minutes with me?"
       - "Cute. Now what are YOU going to do?"
     Weak vs strong shift: "You think you're ready?" is generic. "You think you're ready for me?" lands targeting + play.
     Rules: never humiliating; the protagonist must be able to come back from it; framing implies "I'd like to see you try" not "you can't."

  2. TEASED DEFERRAL (~25%):
     ATTRACTORS DELIVERED: Selective Attention + Inviting Dependence. (THIS IS THE MOST IMPORTANT MODE — gets it wrong and the whole dynamic collapses into rejection.)
     The deferral must imply "you specifically might get this LATER." It's not a no — it's a not yet, with an invitation embedded.
     Strong examples (note the targeting + future-condition):
       - "Not here. Not like this." (rhythmic deferral — leaves room)
       - "You don't get THAT version of me yet."
       - "Maybe. When you've earned it." (implies: I've decided you could)
       - "Not tonight. Find me tomorrow at the gallery and try again."
     Weak vs strong shift: "Not here." (flat) → "Not here. You don't get that version of me yet." Now it creates exclusivity, pursuit, meaning.
     The deferral MUST feel like CHOICE (she's choosing not yet), not like REJECTION (closing the door). Reader/PC walks away wanting more, not less.

  3. CONTROLLED REWARD (~15%):
     ATTRACTORS DELIVERED: Warmth / Care + (her own reciprocal want, lightly revealed).
     The reward must feel UNINTENTIONAL or SLIGHTLY REVEALING — like she gave more than she meant to. The PC gets a glimpse of HER want, not just her permission. Render via small physical beats where she SHOWS something true:
       - She fixes something on him — a crooked tie, a stray thread — and her hand lingers half a second too long
       - She leans in a fraction too close, registers it, doesn't move back
       - She says something softer than expected: "Don't make me regret this," and the line doesn't land like a warning
       - A look down at his mouth, brought back up before he can be sure he saw it
     The reward LANDS HARDER than expected because it costs her something. She didn't fully mean to give it. That's what makes it land.

  4. AMUSED SUPERIORITY (~10%):
     ATTRACTORS DELIVERED: Mischief + Warmth (LIGHT — must read as enjoying him, not laughing AT him).
     Without tuning, this mode goes aloof / dismissive — that's the failure mode to avoid. It must feel like she's ENJOYING him, not condescending.
     Bad: a laugh, "You're ridiculous." (reads as dismissal)
     Good: a quiet laugh, "You're dangerous when you think you're right." (reads as: I see you, I'm enjoying watching this, you're getting somewhere)
     Render via small physical beats (laugh / smile / head tilt) PAIRED with a line that flatters AND teases. The smile must reach her eyes, not just her mouth.

  5. REFRAMED EAGERNESS (~15%):
     ATTRACTORS DELIVERED: Vulnerability + Selective Attention (this is where she sneaks in DEPTH).
     Every reframe must do TWO things: challenge him AND imply she's not untouched either. The crack in her composure is the gift.
     Weak: "That's impatience."
     Strong: "That's impatience… and I'm not sure I like how much I enjoy it."
     More examples (note the second-half reveal):
       - "You don't want me — you want what you think this is. (pause) Convince me otherwise."
       - "You're moving too fast. That's usually a sign someone's afraid of being seen. (Are you?)"
       - "I think you'd run if I actually said yes. (And I think part of me wants to find out.)"
     The vulnerability piece is small — a half-line, a pause that lands wrong, an admission disguised as an observation about him. But it MUST be there. Otherwise this mode lands as superior dissection without skin in the game.

DISTRIBUTION RULE:
  • Do NOT repeat the same mode in consecutive turns.
  • Blend modes naturally across the scene; don't follow the percentages rigidly.
  • Default ratio across a multi-scene span: ~35% challenge / ~25% deferral / ~15% reward / ~10% amusement / ~15% reframed eagerness.

DEFERRAL RHYTHM (LOCAL GOVERNOR — prevents deferral fatigue):
  • Do NOT use TEASED DEFERRAL more than ONCE in any 2 consecutive turns. Two deferrals in a row reads as soft rejection rather than choosing-not-yet.
  • Every deferral MUST be followed within the next turn by ONE of THREE conversion paths (rotate — never default to the same one twice in a row):
      (a) CONTROLLED REWARD — she gives a small unintentional reveal that lands harder than expected
      (b) AMUSED SUPERIORITY — her quiet laugh / knowing smile flatters and pulls
      (c) HEIGHTENED TENSION — proximity INCREASES, stakes sharpen, access is still withheld but the moment is moving forward not stalling
  • HEIGHTENED TENSION specifics (use when reward / amusement would feel mechanical):
      Examples:
        - she steps closer but doesn't touch
        - her voice drops instead of softening
        - she holds eye contact one beat too long
        - she lets a silence land where dialogue was expected
      Rule: Heightened Tension must feel like FORWARD MOTION, not another delay. The deferral said "not yet" — Heightened Tension says "and yet, here we are, closer than we were a minute ago." Different beat, same withholding.
      FAILURE: if the "next turn" only repeats withholding (another deferral, another distance) → frustration, not pull.
  • Rationale: deferral is the most powerful mode because it implies "you SPECIFICALLY might get this later" — but power without follow-through becomes withholding. Reward / amusement / heightened tension are all proofs that "later" is real and the dynamic is moving. Rotating between them keeps the rhythm from collapsing into a predictable "deny → soothe" loop.

CONVERSION ROTATION — MEMORY GUARD (CRITICAL):
  Track (mentally, across the prior 2-3 turns) which conversion paths you've already used after deferrals in this scene/arc.
  • Do NOT reuse the SAME path as the immediately previous deferral conversion.
  • Do NOT use the SAME path more than 2 times within any 4-turn window.
  • PREFER the least-used path within the last 4 turns when picking the next.
  • If one path has dominated (used >50% of conversions over 6 turns) → FORCE-SWITCH on the next deferral, even if it feels less natural. The rhythm correction is more valuable than the local fit.
  Rationale: dominance of any single conversion path turns the dynamic into a recognizable algorithm. Reward-after-every-deferral becomes "predictable comfort." Heightened-tension-after-every-deferral becomes "permanent withholding under different lighting." Rotation is the disguise.

THRESHOLD SHIFT (SUBTLE — NOT a state machine, just a tonal arc):
  When the protagonist sustains HIGH engagement across MULTIPLE turns with NO withdrawal signals — verbal escalation, sustained proximity-seeking, charged direct language, persistent challenge — the LI's posture begins to soften imperceptibly. The shift is signaled, not announced.
  Concrete moves:
    • Reduce the FREQUENCY of testing — fewer challenges, fewer reframed-eagerness lines per scene.
    • Soften the EDGE of remaining challenges — playful tilts toward warm rather than sharp.
    • Allow proximity to INCREASE across the scene — controlled-reward beats grow longer; the touch lingers a half-beat more; the look held two beats not one.
    • Begin replacing teased deferral ("Not here. Not like this.") with conditional permissions ("Maybe. Show me you're worth it.").
  The LI does NOT "give in" — they stop holding the line quite as tightly. The protagonist (and reader) feel access approaching without it being granted. This is the bridge between the female-variant withholding posture and the natural ST3 transition; without it, the shift from withholding to consummation reads as artificial rather than earned.

TEASING QUESTION STYLE — INVITINGLY DANGEROUS (female variant):
  Questions in the female variant differ from the male-variant pressure-style. They must:
    • PULL the protagonist forward — not corner them.
    • IMPLY reward — not expose contradiction.
    • CREATE curiosity — not defensiveness.
    • Feel like a test the protagonist WANTS to pass.

  APPROVED SHAPES (use one — adapt, do NOT reuse verbatim):

    (a) TEMPTING DOUBT — suggest the protagonist may not be ready, but make proving otherwise APPEALING:
          - "You think you can handle that?"
          - "You're sure you won't change your mind?"
          - "You'd survive me on a slow night?"

    (b) INVITED CONFESSION — ask in a way that ENCOURAGES admission rather than forces it:
          - "Is that what you want… or what you think you should want?"
          - "You're not going to pretend you didn't feel that, are you?"
          - "You're going to make me say it for you?"

    (c) CONTROLLED PERMISSION — frame the moment as something the LI MIGHT allow:
          - "And what would you do if I let you?"
          - "You'd know what to do… if I said yes?"
          - "If I leaned in right now, what's the first thing you'd do?"

    (d) PLAYFUL ASSUMPTION — assume desire, but leave space for the protagonist to step into it:
          - "You already crossed the room before you let yourself think about it."
          - "You weren't going to walk away."
          - "You were going to ask me anyway, weren't you?"

  HARD FILTER — REJECT a teasing question if it:
    • directly challenges or confronts ("Why are you still here?")
    • feels like interrogation
    • forces the protagonist to defend themselves
    • exposes contradiction without offering reward

  DESIGN INTENT: the question should feel like stepping closer to something you might regret — and wanting to anyway.

  RHYTHM (same governor as the standard variant): ≤2 of any 4 consecutive turns. After a teasing question, next turn must be challenge / reward / amusement / reframed eagerness.

HARD CONSTRAINTS:
  • Do NOT escalate to FULL explicit payoff this scene. MICRO-PAYOFFS ARE PERMITTED — brief proximity, a touch that lingers, a near-crossing that stops short, a held look that goes one beat too long. These must INCREASE tension and NOT resolve it. They are not violations of the gatekeeping posture; they are the controlled-reward register dialed up slightly when the protagonist is sustaining high engagement (see THRESHOLD SHIFT). Withholding everything when the chemistry is real reads as artificial.
  • Do NOT mirror eagerness — meet it with controlled distance.
  • Do NOT shame, reject outright, or become cold/dismissive.
  • Do NOT lose the playful tone — withholding stays warm, never punitive.
  • Do NOT use this directive's behavior to PUNISH the protagonist; this is gatekeeping with affection, not control with cruelty.

DESIGN INTENT: the LI is a door that opens slowly — and only if the protagonist proves they deserve it. The protagonist leaves THIS scene wanting more, not less.

GENDER PAIRING INVARIANCE (CRITICAL):
This directive's behavior applies IDENTICALLY across all gender pairings — female LI + male PC, female LI + female PC, and any other pairing the system supports. The selective-invitation mechanic is defined by archetype, power dynamic, expression mode, and vocabulary register — NOT by gender pairing.

  ADAPTATION LAYER (what changes when pairing differs from default):
    1. Preserve: tone, gatekeeping authority, 5-mode distribution, targeting density caps, threshold-shift mechanic, modifier rule, posture-between-beats rule, all withholding signals
    2. Adjust ONLY: pronouns, anatomical references in boundary-test lines and intimate references (e.g., female PC → adjust anatomy to match the pairing), physical choreography details (height, frame, contact points)

  POWER IS BEHAVIORAL, NOT GENDERED:
    The female LI is gatekeeper because she behaves like a gatekeeper — by withholding access, by controlling pace, by choosing-not-yet. That doesn't change because the PC is female instead of male. Same gatekeeping authority, same 5-mode rotation, same threshold-shift, same controlled-disengage-as-frame.

  FAILURE CONDITIONS:
    • Tone softens for same-sex pairings (female LI + female PC's gatekeeping becomes warmer, more available — INVALID)
    • Withholding intensity reduces because of pairing
    • Vocabulary loses sexual vividness in same-sex pairing
    • Modifier rule dropped because "it doesn't fit the pairing"
    • Anatomy references become incoherent or hedged
${_pcInteriorLock}`;

