    block += 'CORE DEBATES (express positions — NEVER resolve): leadership vs alignment · privacy vs suspicion · truth vs distortion · consent vs pressure · security vs transparency.\n';
    block += 'CORE TENSIONS (always active in Glass House, no resolution, only pressure):\n';
    GLASS_HOUSE_CANON.coreTensions.forEach(function(t) { block += '  • ' + t + '\n'; });
    block += 'NARRATIVE EFFECT: ' + GLASS_HOUSE_CANON.narrativeEffect + ' In Glass House, visibility is reality — and control over visibility is power.\n';

    // ── Persistent Canon (light, background-real) ──────────────────
    // These figures exist in the world. They are REFERENCED, never
    // INTRODUCED — they surface through dialogue, ambient feeds, or
    // passing mentions, the way real public figures appear in prose.
    block += '\nPERSISTENT CANON (reference, never introduce — no biography, no explanation):\n';
    block += '\n  EXECUTIVE — ' + GLASS_HOUSE_CANON.executive.name + '.\n';
    block += '    Stance: ' + GLASS_HOUSE_CANON.executive.stance + '. Signature line: "' + GLASS_HOUSE_CANON.executive.signature + '". ' + GLASS_HOUSE_CANON.executive.critique + '\n';
    block += '    Usage: ' + GLASS_HOUSE_CANON.executive.usage + '\n';
    block += '\n  INSTITUTIONS (official / technical framing):\n';
    GLASS_HOUSE_CANON.institutions.forEach(function(inst) {
      block += '    • ' + inst.name + ' — ' + inst.role + '. (' + inst.reputation + ')\n';
    });
    block += '\n  INFLUENCERS (feed / clip / quote register; each maps to a voice type):\n';
    GLASS_HOUSE_CANON.influencers.forEach(function(inf) {
      block += '    • ' + inf.handle + ' [' + inf.voice + '] — ' + inf.tone + '.\n';
      block += '      "' + inf.signature + '"' + (inf.alt ? ' / "' + inf.alt + '"' : '') + '\n';
      block += '      Effect: ' + inf.effect + '.\n';
    });
    block += '\n  CULTURAL DRIFT (how alignment moves through the population):\n';
    GLASS_HOUSE_CANON.driftPatterns.forEach(function(p) { block += '    • ' + p + '\n'; });
    block += '\n  REFERENCE DISCIPLINE:\n';
    block += '    - These figures appear through dialogue, ambient feeds, news scrolls, overheard clips, or passing mentions — NEVER as narrator exposition.\n';
    block += '    - Referencing a figure counts as your ONE voice insertion for the scene.\n';
    block += '    - Do NOT invoke the same figure back-to-back across scenes. If you referenced Sera Vale last scene, this scene picks someone else — or no one.\n';
    block += '    - Do NOT explain who they are. A name and a fragment is enough: "Hexa clipped it already." / "Cal Rho would eat this alive." / "The CSA flagged the feed." The reader fills in.\n';
    block += '    - Match the figure to the voice type you selected. Cal Rho → institutional. Sera Vale / Luma Thread → media. Devin Kade / Hexa → cultural.\n';
    block += '    - President Ellison is a SPECIAL CASE: allusions to partial alignment, broadcast-only participation, or public controversy are permitted across voice types — never settle the debate.\n';
    block += 'HARD RULES:\n';
    block += '  - NEVER explain The Chorus, define its laws, or settle a debate. Positions only.\n';
    block += '  - NEVER stack voices in one scene — the selected voice above is the only voice this scene may use.\n';
    block += '  - NEVER repeat the phrasing of a prior scene\'s insertion. If nothing fresh comes, OMIT.\n';
    block += '  - Max length: one short line or clause. Never a paragraph, never an aside.\n';
    block += '  - If it reads like exposition, resolves a debate, or breaks pacing, REMOVE IT.\n';
