      return '\n\nANCESTRY (HARD — Roman 2026-06-11 contemporary-world contract): ' +
        'the player explicitly chose ANCESTRY = "' + ancestry + '" for ' + labelLong + '. This is a HERITAGE signal, not a worldbuilding instruction.\n' +
        '  • SETTING REMAINS UNCHANGED — the story stays in ' + worldLabel + '. Do NOT relocate; do NOT make this a story "about being ' + ancestry + '" — it is a story in this world starring someone of ' + ancestry + ' heritage.\n' +
        '  • APPEARANCE COHERENCE — the "complexion", "hair", "face", and "signature_feature" fields below should reflect ' + ancestry + ' heritage. Real-person specificity, NEVER stereotype.\n' +
        '  • NAMING — ' + labelLong + '\'s given + family name should plausibly come from ' + ancestry + ' tradition (or a generationally-blended version if her/his family is established in the setting). If the player picked a name that does NOT match the ancestry, HONOR the picked name and let the mismatch imply ONE plausible character history (immigrant generation, adopted, mixed-heritage parents, married name) — let the Bible imply it naturally; do NOT belabor.\n' +
        '  • CULTURAL MEMORY (LIGHT TOUCH) — small specific details may surface in the prose across an Issue: a remembered grandmother\'s saying, a food smell, a holiday observance, a phrase in another language used unselfconsciously, a religious or seasonal tradition. ONE such detail per Issue is plenty. Do NOT turn the character into a heritage exhibit.\n' +
        '  • FORBIDDEN — (1) changing the setting / world; (2) making the story PRIMARILY about ' + pronoun + ' identity (heritage informs the character; it is not the plot); (3) over-explaining ' + pronoun + ' background as plot exposition; (4) reducing ' + pronoun + ' to representational tokens of ' + ancestry + ' culture; (5) ignoring the ancestry entirely (the appearance fields + at least one named cultural-memory detail per Issue carry it).\n';
