  // the A-plot prompt; this is the scene-level enforcement layer.
  function buildGoalRelationshipPrincipleDirective() {
    // Skip if no A-plot has been initialized yet (story setup phase).
    if (!state || !state.aPlot) return '';
    var _relShaping = state.aPlot.relational_shaping || '';
    return '\n\nGOAL ↔ RELATIONSHIP RECIPROCITY + CONDUCTIVITY (HARD — scene-level enforcement):\n' +
      'This story sustains TWO INDEPENDENT ENGINES: an A-plot the reader cares about IN ITSELF (the external conflict matters regardless of romance) AND an R-plot the reader cares about IN ITSELF (the people emotionally surviving each other matters regardless of mission). Gold standard: Han + Leia vs the Empire — reader cares about BOTH outcomes.\n' +
      '\n' +
      'THIS SCENE\'S DECISIONS MUST AFFECT BOTH ENGINES. A scene that only advances the A-plot (mission step, info reveal, tactical move) without ALSO shifting the R-plot (trust, distance, vulnerability, attachment) is HALF A SCENE. A scene that only advances the R-plot (confession, closeness, distance) without ALSO touching the A-plot (mission consequence, leverage shift, stake adjustment) is also HALF A SCENE. The reciprocity is the architecture.\n' +
      '\n' +
      'CONDUCTIVITY TEST (per-scene, ASK YOURSELF before emitting):\n' +
      '  What does THIS scene\'s external pressure produce emotionally? NOT "what happened" (logistical) but "what REVELATION or REPOSITIONING did the pressure produce?" If the answer is "the mission step happened" — the scene is INERT.\n' +
      '  AMPLITUDE: conductivity is a SPECTRUM. HEAVY (trust fracture / moral collision / sacrifice crisis) is for true peaks — reserve. MEDIUM (visible admiration / leverage shift / boundary tested) is the working register. LIGHT (subtle admiration noticed-but-unnamed / micro-irritation / ambient longing / shared competence rhythm / practical coordination becoming intimacy of capability) is necessary BREATH — without it, heavy beats stack and lose punch. Match scene amplitude to its position in the arc. Do NOT artificially inflate a coordination beat into a moral collision; that reads as forced significance.\n' +
      '\n' +
      'COMPETENCE IS EMOTIONAL: romance readers engage with A-plots through capability — leadership, sacrifice, strategic intelligence, resilience, courage under pressure, loyalty proven through action. Characters become MORE lovable through how they ACT under pressure (what they protect, sacrifice, hide, refuse to abandon). Design scene beats that create competence-attraction alongside vulnerability-attraction.\n' +
      '\n' +
      'BAD INTEGRATION (HARD FAILS): "now mission, now kissing" (alternation); romance detachable from plot; external conflict replaceable without emotional consequence; romance reduced to occasional reward scenes between mission beats; "informational" or "bureaucratic" scenes with no emotional revelation.\n' +
      '\n' +
      'GOOD INTEGRATION: external pressure reveals character truth; emotional evolution alters strategic outcomes; a single decision creates BOTH strategic gain AND emotional cost (or vice versa); sacrifice solves one layer while wounding another; competence under pressure becomes attraction; pragmatic morally-grey choices become trust fractures.\n' +
      (_relShaping ? '\nA-PLOT RELATIONAL SHAPING (from story setup — honor this in scene choices): ' + _relShaping + '\n' : '');
  }
  window.buildGoalRelationshipPrincipleDirective = buildGoalRelationshipPrincipleDirective;

  // ── ORIENTATION-AWARE ADAPTATIONS (2026-05-23, gap #3) ─────────
  // The user has an attentional gravity preference between GOAL and
  // RELATIONSHIP, tracked via microDecisions → state._stagedPreferenceAxis
  // → _getUnifiedAxisTally. Per the user's authoritative direction,
  // this preference should NOT branch the story (both engines remain
  // real and compelling). It changes EMPHASIS across 5 dimensions:
  //
  //   1. SCENE WEIGHTING — what gets more prose minutes
  //   2. CLIFFHANGER TYPE — external escalation vs emotional rupture
  //   3. EMOTIONAL REVELATION MODE — through action vs through interpretation
  //   4. CALLBACK EMPHASIS — promises/sacrifices vs phrases/touches
  //   5. A-PLOT FRAMING — world-stakes foregrounded vs framed through emotional consequence
  //
