        '\nPROSE REALITY CHECK (HARD): Use ONLY people, objects, and surfaces that actually appear in the RECENT SCENE TEXT. Do NOT invent props (a table, chair, desk, etc.) the scene has not established. Match the real setting and who is actually present.\n' +
        '\nDECK EXCLUSION (HARD): NEVER reference the protagonist\'s tarot deck, the cards, the Marseille deck, or "let the cards decide / draw a card" in ANY action or dialogue — even though the deck appears in the scene. Drawing a card is a SEPARATE player mechanic, NOT a Say/Do option; keep the deck entirely out of the suggestion (no "push the deck aside," no "I\'m deciding this, not the cards").\n' +
        '\nPHYSICAL PRESENCE vs MENTION (HARD — read the scene ENDING): a character being NAMED or referenced is NOT proof they are physically present. References to an ABSENT figure — "his shadow loomed," "Roman Tusk\'s influence," "she thought of him," "dancing to his tune" — mean that person is NOT in the room. Judge who is present by the scene\'s STAGING at its END. If the scene ENDS with the protagonist ALONE (they walked away, left, are moving / en route, or no other character shares the immediate space), the cards MUST be about the protagonist\'s OWN next move, inner stance, or situation. Do NOT suggest blocking, confronting, stepping toward, or addressing a character who is not physically in the scene at that moment ("step into his path" / "meet his eyes" when he is elsewhere is INVALID).\n';
