              'Curiosity-before-collision is correct: Scene 1 establishes HER life, HER wound, the SHAPE of the absence — before the LI arrives. ' + _liNameSc1 + ' does NOT need to be on the page in present time. But archetype is a VERB, and Scene 1 must show both archetypes acting on SOME surface available to it. Off-page is fully sufficient if the surfaces are doing archetype work.\n' +
              '\n' +
              'TWO INDEPENDENT SURFACES where archetype must manifest in Scene 1:\n' +
              '\n' +
              '(1) THE NARRATOR\'S OWN ARCHETYPE — in the prose texture itself, regardless of whether ' + _liNameSc1 + ' is on-page. This is fully available even when the LI is entirely absent.\n' +
              '  • OPEN_VEIN narrator: she keeps arriving at an emotional conclusion BEFORE the evidence justifies it. The prose moves inward before it moves outward. Example (LI absent from the line): "Sophie looked thinner than I remembered. My stomach tightened before I understood why." The archetype manifested. ' + _liNameSc1 + ' was nowhere in the sentence.\n' +
              '  • ARMORED_FOX narrator: every serious moment becomes a joke half a second before it lands. Her observations carry a hairline crack of wit-as-armor. Example: a sober paragraph that ends with a perfectly-timed sidestep.\n' +
              '  • HEART_WARDEN narrator: she registers who in the room is on their feet too long, who has not been asked, what is being undertended. Example: she walks into a deposition and the first thing she notices is that opposing counsel has not eaten.\n' +
              '  • SPELLBINDER narrator: she registers gravity — three different people stopping mid-sentence, the geometry of attention bending — and is partly registering her own pull. Example: noticing who-watches-whom in a room ' + _liNameSc1 + ' has not entered.\n' +
              '  This surface is HARD: the narrator\'s archetype must be legible on multiple observations across Scene 1, not just one. If you stripped every mention of the LI from the page, the narrator\'s archetype should still be identifiable.\n' +
              '\n' +
              '(2) THE LI\'S ARCHETYPE — in every surface he appears on, off-page. NOT in present-time dialogue (that is later). In remembered quoted lines, anecdotes she carries, what mentors/witnesses say about him, voicemails, third-party stories, photographs, inferences. EACH appearance is a surface that must be doing archetype work.\n' +
              '  • What ' + _liNameSc1 + '\'s archetype demands in Scene 1 (the VERB shape, even off-page):\n' +
              '    ' + (_ARCHETYPE_SCENE1_BEHAVIOR[_archId] || _archId + ': express archetype through what he says, does, or has said — even in remembered or reported form.') + '\n' +
              '  • ARMORED FOX worked example. A FAILURE shape (observed-live): a remembered line "She made her choice." — Fox-neutral, stoic-billionaire. A WORKING shape (deflection that accidentally leaks the wound): "Well, that\'s one way to avoid Christmas shopping" — and later she realizes he was talking about his sister leaving. Or: "If she wanted to disappear properly, Montauk was an amateur choice." Or: "You know what the annoying thing is? She stole the good coffee grinder." Each is Fox: deflection in shape, wound underneath, never confessional. ' + _liNameSc1 + ' was never on the page in present time — and his archetype is fully visible.\n' +
              '  • OPEN_VEIN LI off-page: a remembered line where he NAMED a fear before she\'d known him long enough to ask. A voicemail that leaks feeling without performing it. An anecdote a third party shares: "He cried at his secretary\'s retirement party. We all stood there." The vulnerability arrives un-armored even through someone else\'s mouth.\n' +
              '  • HEART_WARDEN LI off-page: the doorman remembers him walking her to her car for two years without making it a story. A note he left tucked under something. The thing he did once, never mentioned again.\n' +
              '  • SPELLBINDER LI off-page: a description of a room going still when his name comes up. The way a colleague stops talking mid-sentence at his approach (described by someone else).\n' +
              '  • DARK_VICE LI off-page: a remembered line where he named what she had not let herself say. A voicemail that delivers permission disguised as recognition. A third-party reporting "he asked me the one question I had been avoiding all year."\n' +
              '  • BEAUTIFUL_RUIN LI off-page: a remembered kindness that carried a test underneath it. An anecdote: "he failed a test of his own once and never recovered from the fact that someone had seen it."\n' +
              '  • ETERNAL_FLAME LI off-page: a third party who recalls how long he has been waiting. A photograph with a date that means more to him than it does to anyone else.\n' +
              '\n' +
              'FORBIDDEN substitutes (these are the OBSERVED FAILURE MODES — Sonnet keeps reaching for them):\n' +
              '  • Dossier copy: ' + _liNameSc1 + ' reduced to file summary (age, education, family, holdings) with no archetype-shaped speech, behavior, or remembered moment anywhere on the page.\n' +
              '  • Stoic-billionaire furniture: the watch, the scar, the house, the stillness, the broad shoulders — used as a substitute for archetype-shaped behavior. These are physical evidence at best; they are NOT manifestation.\n' +
              '  • Fox-NEUTRAL remembered lines: "She made her choice." / "I made my decision." / "That was the end of it." — stoic, declarative, archetype-invisible. If you imported a remembered line that any archetype could have spoken, you imported a wristwatch.\n' +
              '  • Mentor-secondhand summary that strips archetype: "He was respected. He was difficult. He was alone." — three sentences that say nothing archetype-specific. Mentor reportage must itself be archetype-shaped (the mentor reporting a Fox-shaped joke, an Open Vein moment that disarmed her, a Heart Warden act that no one was supposed to see).\n' +
              '\n' +
              'BRUTAL TEST. After Scene 1, an editor should be able to identify: (a) the PC\'s archetype from the narration texture alone (even with every LI mention deleted); (b) the LI\'s archetype from every surface he appears on (each remembered line / anecdote / inference is archetype-shaped). If either fails, archetype is metadata, not on the page.\n' +
              '\n' +
              'NO ARCHETYPE DILUTION. Manifestation can be quiet, brief, indirect — but it must BE there. If Scene 1 could function unchanged with a different LI archetype, the archetype is not properly expressed.\n' +
              // ── OPENING ORDER (Roman 2026-06-01) ───────────────────────────
              // The A-plot event does NOT introduce the LI. It REACTIVATES an
              // unresolved emotional reality that already exists. PC opens
              // inside her carrying-of-Roman; the A-plot event arrives
              // MID-PARAGRAPH as the catalyst that makes the carrying
              // inescapable. Roman as PRESENCE first, person second:
              // ghost, question, scar, joke, voicemail, promise, betrayal,
              // kindness she still cannot explain. Before the reader wonders
              // "what happened?" they should wonder "what happened between
              // them?" Makes the A-plot the match, not the fire.
              '\nOPENING ORDER (HARD — Roman 2026-06-01): for Grounded/Orbit openings (' + _liNameSc1 + ' offstage in present time), the FIRST EMOTIONAL QUESTION must precede the first plot question. The A-plot event does NOT introduce ' + _liNameSc1 + ' — it REACTIVATES an unresolved emotional reality that was already happening before the scene started.\n' +
              '\n' +
              'STRUCTURAL ORDERING (HARD — this is the actual beat sequence, not "tone advice"):\n' +
              '  • FIRST ~80–140 words: ' + _liNameSc1 + ' as PRESENCE — not the man, the GRAVITY. A specific Roman-shaped beat she is already carrying: a memory (drawn from his Bible\'s defining_anecdote / secondary_anecdote / signature_behavior), an unfinished thought, a voicemail she has not played all the way through, a sentence he said that she has been holding for years, a joke she remembers and has not yet decided what to do with, a kindness she still cannot explain, a promise, a scar she got from him, an unanswered question. The beat must be ARCHETYPE-SHAPED (Fox-deflective, Open-Vein-undressed, Heart-Warden-protective, etc. — see ARCHETYPE MANIFESTATION above). It must read as something she was ALREADY DOING / FEELING when the scene began, not something that arrives with the A-plot.\n' +
              '  • MID-PARAGRAPH (around words 100–160): the A-plot event arrives. The wire / voicemail / envelope / collapse / phone call / arrival. It interrupts. Crucially, it IMPLIES ' + _liNameSc1 + ' before it names him — the reader connects the gravity already on the page to the event, BEFORE the prose explicitly says "Roman is involved." The connection is implicit and the reader gets there first.\n' +
              '  • REST OF SCENE: she now cannot put down what she had already been carrying. The plot is the force that makes the unresolved emotional reality undeniable.\n' +
              '\n' +
              'WORKED EXAMPLE — Armored Fox + Grounded opening (Roman 2026-06-01, verbatim shape; do NOT reuse this content, only the structural shape):\n' +
              '  "The last voicemail Roman left me was about airline coffee.\n' +
              '   Not the crash. Not the hearing. Not the fact that my career was ending in real time. Airline coffee. Six minutes about why nobody understood extraction temperatures at altitude. One sentence that might have been an apology.\n' +
              '   I spent three years trying to decide.\n' +
              '   The wire transfer arrived at 4:47 a.m."\n' +
              '\n' +
              'Notice the structural moves: (1) opens INSIDE the Roman-gravity (a Fox-shaped remembered voicemail — deflection about coffee instead of the crisis); (2) names the emotional question ("an apology... three years trying to decide"); (3) THEN the A-plot event arrives ("the wire transfer arrived"); (4) the reader immediately understands the wire transfer MEANS Roman without being told. The plot is the match; the fire was already lit.\n' +
              '\n' +
              'FAILURE MODES (the OLD shape you must NOT default to):\n' +
              '  • A-plot event in sentence 1 → Roman-as-puzzle-piece by sentence 4. The reader wonders "what is this thing?" before they wonder "what happened between them?" The order is inverted; ' + _liNameSc1 + ' becomes a node in the plot graph instead of the emotional center.\n' +
              '  • Roman introduced via dossier / file / news clipping / mentor-summary. He arrives as INFORMATION. The opening order is correct only when he arrives as GRAVITY first — something she has been carrying, not something she has been investigating.\n' +
              '  • The Roman-gravity beat is a generic "I was thinking about him" placeholder. The beat must be SPECIFIC — drawn from his Bible\'s behavioral fields, archetype-shaped, with a verb at its center. A generic placeholder is the failure shape one level up.\n' +
              '\n' +
              'BRUTAL TEST: after Scene 1, ask which question the reader has — "what happened?" or "what happened between them?" If the first one comes first, the opening order is inverted. The emotional question must precede the plot question. ' + _liNameSc1 + ' should arrive as ghost / scar / joke / unanswered voicemail BEFORE he arrives as a name in a plot mechanism.\n';
